Linux build notes
=================

This tree keeps the original OpenBSD build files and adds a separate
GNU make build for Linux. On Linux, plain `make` uses `GNUmakefile`.
The original `Makefile` is still present for the OpenBSD-style `bmake`
path.

Dependencies
------------

You need a C compiler, GNU make, curses, and libbsd development headers.
On Debian or Ubuntu-like systems this usually means:

	build-essential libncurses-dev libbsd-dev

Install those with your distribution's package manager; package names may
vary.

Build
-----

Build the local binary:

	make

This first builds `makedefs`, uses it to generate `hack.onames.h` from
`def.objects.h`, and then links `./hack` against curses and libbsd.

Remove generated build output:

	make clean

Local runtime directory
-----------------------

For development runs, use a local game directory instead of system-wide
or privileged install paths:

	make hackdir

This creates `./hackdir`, copies `data`, `help`, `hh`, and `rumors`, and
creates the empty runtime files `record` and `perm`. It also creates
`hackdir/save` for saved games.

Run the game locally:

	make run

This is equivalent to:

	./hack -d ./hackdir

Score listing can be checked without starting an interactive game:

	./hack -d ./hackdir -s

Porting scope
-------------

The Linux port is intended for local development and play. It does not
install a setuid binary, does not create shared system game directories,
and does not install files under `/usr/share` or `/var/games`.

Linux-only compatibility is collected in `linux_compat.h`. Prefer adding
small Linux shims there instead of spreading platform conditionals through
gameplay code.

Generated and runtime files
---------------------------

The following files are generated locally and should not be committed:

	hack
	makedefs
	hack.onames.h
	*.o
	hackdir/
	record
	perm
	record_lock
	safelock
	save/

The top-level `record` and `perm` files may appear if you accidentally
run `./hack` without `-d ./hackdir`; remove them or leave them ignored.
